cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Laguna Genealogies: A Digital Edition of Parsons 1923"
message: >-
  If you use this edition, please cite both it and the 1923 publication it
  transcribes (see references).
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Heger-Vlahovic
    given-names: Elizabeth
abstract: >-
  A verified digital transcription of the genealogical plates published with
  Elsie Clews Parsons's "Laguna Genealogies" (1923). All four plates are edited
  here, 713 individuals in all. Table 1, "Genealogy I", records 104 individuals
  across five generations, with 27 marriages and 80 parent-child links. Table 2,
  "Genealogy II", records 275 individuals across six generations, with 61
  marriages and 214 parent-child links, in three descent blocks. Table 3,
  "Genealogy III", records 261 individuals across seven generations, with 72
  marriages and 192 parent-child links, in two descent blocks. Table 4,
  "Genealogy IV", records 73 individuals across four generations, with 14
  marriages and 58 parent-child links. All are transcribed character by
  character including Americanist phonetic diacritics, and encoded as a
  normalised persons/unions/children schema. Clan descent is matrilineal, which
  gives a structural check on the bracket readings, though only where the
  candidate mothers belong to different clans; elsewhere the evidence is the
  plate's own bracket geometry.
keywords:
  - Laguna Pueblo
  - genealogy
  - Elsie Clews Parsons
  - Keresan
  - digital edition
  - Southwest anthropology
license: CC-BY-4.0
# There is deliberately no "doi:" field and no "identifiers:" block. Both held
# Zenodo dois -- the concept doi and v1.0.0's version doi -- until 2026-08-08,
# when the deposit was deleted and the archive withdrawn from the edition's
# public face. Both dois now return 410 Gone, so restoring either here would
# advertise a citation that no reader can resolve. The deposit webhook is off
# and no further release is cut. This file is what GitHub renders as the repo's
# "Cite this repository" widget, so it is the most visible place a doi could
# reappear. See CLAUDE.md -> Exposure posture.
repository-code: "https://github.com/PuebloGenealogy/pueblogenealogy.github.io"
url: "https://pueblogenealogy.github.io/"
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Laguna Genealogies"
    authors:
      - family-names: Parsons
        given-names: Elsie Clews
    year: 1923
    journal: "Anthropological Papers of the American Museum of Natural History"
    volume: "19"
    issue: "5"
    start: 133
    end: 292
    publisher:
      name: "American Museum of Natural History"
    location:
      name: "New York"
